            specVersion: 2,
            specPath: "spec/components/Divider.yaml",
            isPattern: false,
            platforms: [("ios", .full), ("ipados", .full), ("macos", .full), ("watchos", .none), ("web-touch", .full), ("web-desktop", .full)],
            implemented: [("ios", 2), ("ipados", 2), ("macos", 2)],
            platformNotes: [("ios", "`isDecorative: false` has no Apple counterpart: SwiftUI's accessibility traits carry no separator, and a Divider speaks no word of its own (ADR-0032 rules 1 and 5), so on iOS, iPadOS and macOS the line is never an accessibility element, whatever `isDecorative` says. Where a rule is the only grouping cue, a container or a heading carries the grouping for VoiceOver; the web exposes the same line as `role=\"separator\"`. Behavior 2's parent that sizes to its content is, in SwiftUI, a stack under `fixedSize` on the Divider's cross axis: `.fixedSize(horizontal: true, vertical: false)` around a horizontal Divider's `VStack`, `.fixedSize(horizontal: false, vertical: true)` around a vertical one's `HStack`. The Divider's ideal length is zero, so there it is as long as the longest other child, as in the web's shrink-to-fit box and flex row. A stack left to size itself is not such a parent: the Divider is flexible along its length, so the stack takes all the length it is offered, and the Divider runs that."), ("watchos", "`none`. ADR-0010's Tier 3 list does not name Divider, and a watch screen is a vertical stack of at most three glanceable tiles (visual-dna §6.3), where spacing and the luminance ladder separate. A white-8 % hairline there would be structure for sighted users only (visual-dna §7.5). This is a decision, not a gap: the watch uses the gap tokens instead."), ("macos", "identical to iOS; a Divider has no hover state, because it is not interactive.")],
