            platformNotes: [("watchos", "full, and the only reason this note exists is the Tier 3 list: Icon is one of the nine components ADR-0010 puts on the wrist, and every prop works there because the registry's Apple binding carries the wrist as it carries the phone (ADR-0013 rule 3). Guidance only: the watch's glanceable stack reaches for the sm and md boxes, and `weight: display` rarely earns its place on a 45 mm screen."), ("macos", "No Bold Text setting, so the weight ladder never steps (ADR-0021 §3); density is the lever that changes the control around the glyph, not the glyph."), ("web-touch", "Right to left (behavior 11), read by the same stylesheet as on web-desktop, whose note gives the reasons: a glyph whose registry entry sets `rtlMirror.web` flips only under an explicit `dir=\"rtl\"` attribute on the glyph or an ancestor. The nearest `dir` of `rtl` or `ltr` decides, exact through four nested changes of direction; `dir=\"auto\"` keeps the surrounding direction, and a direction set any other way is not seen."), ("web-desktop", "Phosphor cuts are bundled as SVG from the generated registry map; no SF Symbol name ever reaches the web package, the gallery or the token export (ADR-0013 rule 5). Right to left (behavior 11): a glyph whose registry entry sets `rtlMirror.web` flips only under an explicit `dir=\"rtl\"` attribute on the glyph or an ancestor. The nearest `dir` attribute whose value is `rtl` or `ltr`, in any letter case, decides, and that reading is exact through four nested changes of direction (rtl, ltr, rtl, ltr, counted from the outermost rtl down); a glyph under more changes than that draws unmirrored. `dir=\"auto\"`, like a missing or invalid value, keeps the surrounding direction rather than the one its text resolves to, and a direction set any other way than by a `dir` attribute, such as the CSS `direction` property, is not seen. `:dir(rtl)` would have followed `dir=\"auto\"` and nesting of any depth, and does in Chrome 120 and later, Safari and Firefox, but it fails at Prism's browser floor of Chrome 111, which lacks it, and a consumer's build aimed at that floor rewrites it into `:lang()` selectors that a `dir` attribute never matches (P5-3 finding SD-7). So the package reads the attribute, with selectors no build at the floor rewrites (`Glyph.css`). Apple is unaffected: it reads the environment's `layoutDirection`.")],
