    static let specAdaptiveShell: DSComponentEntry = DSComponentEntry(
            name: "AdaptiveShell",
            layer: "pattern",
            summary: "The frame a Prism app lives in, and the one place that knows which chrome belongs to which width. It stacks on a phone (a TopBar above the content and a floating TabBar over its bottom), puts a Sidebar beside the content or a row of PillTabs in the TopBar from tablet width, and adds a CommandPalette and a trailing detail region at desktop width. Layout follows the container width; hover, tooltips and hit size follow input modality, so a tablet with a trackpad keeps its layout and gains its pointer affordances.",
            since: "0.1.0",
            specVersion: 1,
            specPath: "spec/patterns/AdaptiveShell.yaml",
            isPattern: true,
            platforms: [("ios", .adapted), ("ipados", .full), ("macos", .adapted), ("watchos", .none), ("web-touch", .adapted), ("web-desktop", .full)],
            implemented: [],
            platformNotes: [("ios", "adapted — `stack` at every width. The TabBar is the system tab bar, which Prism hands the destinations and the selection and does not paint (TabBar.yaml, ADR-0009 decision 1). An iPhone in landscape is wider than 600 pt, but Sidebar is `ios: none` and PillTabs on a phone are section tabs rather than primary navigation (PillTabs.yaml), so `layout: auto` resolves to `stack` there, and a forced `split` or `sidebar` does too. With no desktop width there is no detail region; a detail opens as a sheet."), ("ipados", "full — all three layouts by width: `stack` in a narrow multitasking window, `split` in portrait and `sidebar` from 1024 px, which every iPad reaches in landscape and where the detail region appears; modality flips to pointer while a trackpad is connected and the layout does not change (ADR-0010, ADR-0019 §2)."), ("macos", "adapted — compact density, pointer modality, and the Sidebar, CommandPalette and ContextMenu of ADR-0010 Tier 2. A Mac never takes `stack`, because TabBar is `macos: none`: a window below 600 px takes `split` with the Sidebar as its rail (or PillTabs, when asked), from 600 px `split`, and from 1024 px `sidebar`; a forced `stack` resolves to `split`."), ("watchos", "none — ADR-0010 Tier 3 has no TabBar, Sidebar, PillTabs, TopBar, CommandPalette or Tooltip, and the watch navigates with the system's own navigation, so there is no chrome for a shell to arrange. A pattern is a recipe rather than an implementation (ADR-0012 rule 3), and a recipe that composes nothing has nothing to say; a watch screen follows DetailScreen's watch form, a title and a vertical stack of at most three glanceable tiles (spec/patterns/README.md)."), ("web-touch", "adapted — every layout by container width, exactly as on web-desktop, because the layout follows the container and not the platform. What differs is the modality: touch means no hover state, no tooltips, no ContextMenu, no CommandPalette (`web-touch: none` in its own spec) and size.hit at 44. The web chooses density from the presence of a coarse pointer and modality from whether the primary input can hover finely (ADR-0019 §2), so a touchscreen laptop gets the regular density with pointer affordances, and this row's restrictions lift.")],
